Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?feat(pulse):=20multi-property=20=E2=80=94=20one?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20hotel=20per=20session,=20scoped=20everywhere?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Pulse was single-property: 1 of 278 tables carried id_hotel. This makes a
hotel the unit every screen, query and job works in.

Signing in
  A new pulsehotel module gates every Pulse screen. An employee with grants
  for more than one property picks one at sign-in; with exactly one, it is
  chosen silently; with none, the session is ended with an explanation
  rather than left on an empty desk. Grants are per employee
  (pulse_hotel_access), with QloApps' profile->hotel table honoured as a
  fallback until an install has migrated. A revoked grant bites on the next
  click, not the next login. The top bar carries a switcher.

Scoping
  Rather than add a predicate to 2,506 hand-written queries, every Pulse
  query now goes through PulseDb, and PulseHotelScope rewrites the SQL:
  each scoped table named in a statement gets its hotel predicate, in the
  ON clause for an outer join so a LEFT JOIN does not quietly become an
  INNER one, and inside each subquery in its own right. Writes are stamped
  on the way in. With no hotel chosen the predicate matches nothing, never
  everything, and an unstamped insert is refused rather than written into
  no property at all.

  Verified against 15,199 distinct statements captured from a real run:
  15,112 rewritten, every one accepted by MySQL, none broken. Screens,
  crons, the JSON API and the public portals were then re-run per property;
  no statement reads another hotel's rows except four that say in the code
  why they are group-wide (the licence room cap, the group settings
  fallback, and the setup screen's own survey of which properties are
  unseeded).

Schema
  268 tables gain id_hotel and an index; 141 unique keys and 22 natural
  primary keys are widened to include it, so two properties can each have a
  charge code called ROOM and each start their journal numbering at 1.
  modules/pulsehotel/migrate.php does this to an existing install and
  back-fills the rows from the room, the booking or the parent record.

Sessions that have no session
  Crons work through the properties one at a time. An API token belongs to
  a property, and a group token must name one per request. Public pages
  take the hotel from what the request already proves — a paired screen, a
  booking token, a payslip link — and refuse rather than guess.

Reference data
  A property owns its charge codes, accounts, menu and pay elements, so a
  new one starts empty and cannot trade. PulseHotelSetup copies the setup
  across from a running property, keeping parent references pointed at the
  copies, and brings nothing transactional: no folios, no journals, no
  staff, no guests.

Also fixed, found while doing this: the payroll seed row that broke the
accounts INSERT (wrong column count, and pointing at a casual-labour
account instead of A&G payroll), and a POS API resource that refused a
valid bearer token.

